        ":question/problem": "![](images/4b/4b91ee95f42770036434909f2bb62102120f85f464e548a0142a603164a41b32.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x = 0$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n2. $x =-1$ and $x = 2$ are roots of even multiplicity.\n3. $p(x) → ∞$ as $x → ∞$."
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233151,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
        },
        ":question/id": "22ff9dcd-4fa1-5c0a-bb5b-2eec3be880ce",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-112591",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/7b/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."
      },
