        ":question/problem": "![](images/1f/1f49d3448340c612269cdeea20680d717c736cb9a19fcf83dac86da1e4a6127f.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a simple root.\n- $x=2$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-1$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=2$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."
